(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[23] = {"n":23,"set":"C","level":"Exam Standard","band":"B1 25% · B2 50% · B2+ 25%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"Biomimicry in engineering","p":["Nature has been solving engineering problems for nearly four billion years. Through evolution, living things have developed structures and systems that are efficient, durable and often remarkably economical in their use of energy and materials. Biomimicry, sometimes called biomimetics, is the practice of studying these natural solutions and adapting them to human design. Rather than asking what we can take from nature, biomimicry asks what we can learn from it.","One of the best-known examples began with a walk in the countryside. In the 1940s, the Swiss engineer George de Mestral noticed that small burrs, the seed cases of certain plants, kept sticking to his clothes and to his dog's fur. Examining them under a microscope, he saw that they were covered in tiny hooks that caught on loops of fabric or hair. After years of experiments, he developed a fastener with hooks on one side and loops on the other. The result, Velcro, is now used in everything from children's shoes to spacecraft.","Transport engineers have also borrowed ideas from animals. When Japan's high-speed Shinkansen trains exited tunnels at high speed, they produced a loud boom caused by compressed air, disturbing nearby residents. Eiji Nakatsu, an engineer who was also a keen birdwatcher, observed that the kingfisher dives from the air into water with hardly a splash, thanks to its long, narrow beak. Redesigning the front of the train to resemble the bird's beak not only reduced the noise but also allowed the train to use about fifteen per cent less electricity while travelling faster.","Buildings, too, can learn from nature. In the hot climate of Zimbabwe, the architect Mick Pearce designed the Eastgate Centre, a large office and shopping complex, using principles inspired by termite mounds. Termites maintain a fairly stable temperature inside their mounds through a system of channels that allow warm air to escape and cooler air to enter. Eastgate uses a similar system of vents and chimneys and consumes far less energy for cooling than conventional buildings of a similar size. Such approaches are of obvious interest in the Gulf region, where cooling accounts for a very large share of electricity use.","Other examples come from the surfaces of plants and animals. The leaves of the lotus plant stay clean because microscopic bumps on their surface cause water to form droplets that roll off, carrying dirt with them. This 'lotus effect' has inspired self-cleaning paints and glass. Similarly, the skin of sharks is covered in tiny tooth-like scales that reduce drag and discourage bacteria and small organisms from attaching. Researchers have developed surfaces based on shark skin for ships and even for hospital equipment.","Despite its successes, biomimicry is not simply a matter of copying. Natural structures usually work at small scales and depend on complex combinations of materials that are difficult to reproduce in factories. Engineers must understand the underlying principle rather than merely imitating appearance. Moreover, some critics point out that a product inspired by nature is not automatically environmentally friendly. Nevertheless, as societies search for ways to use fewer resources, biomimicry is likely to become an increasingly valuable source of ideas, reminding us that some of the best designs have already been tested by nature."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how animals adapt to hot climates","how engineers learn from designs found in nature","the history of Japanese trains","why plants grow in different shapes"],"k":1,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage gives examples of biomimicry.","h":"التعلم من الطبيعة في الهندسة."},{"n":2,"s":"How did George de Mestral get the idea for Velcro?","o":["by examining burrs that stuck to his clothes","by studying the feet of insects","by watching birds dive into water","by designing spacecraft"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"من البذور الشائكة."},{"n":3,"s":"What problem did the Shinkansen trains originally cause?","o":["frequent accidents","very slow journeys","a loud noise when leaving tunnels","damage to the railway lines"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 describes the boom.","h":"ضوضاء عالية."},{"n":4,"s":"Why does the writer mention that Nakatsu was a keen birdwatcher?","o":["to show that he was not a real engineer","to describe his free time in detail","to explain how he came to notice the kingfisher's design","to suggest that birds are dangerous to trains"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"His hobby led to the idea.","h":"تفسير مصدر الفكرة."},{"n":5,"s":"The word 'conventional' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["ordinary or traditional","very modern","extremely large","badly designed"],"k":0,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Ordinary buildings of similar size.","h":"تقليدي."},{"n":6,"s":"What can be inferred about designs like the Eastgate Centre?","o":["They can only be built in Zimbabwe.","They could help reduce energy use in hot countries such as those in the Gulf.","They require more electricity than other buildings.","They are suitable only for termites."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"Cooling uses much electricity in the Gulf.","h":"مفيدة لدول الخليج."},{"n":7,"s":"How do lotus leaves stay clean?","o":["They produce a natural soap.","Insects clean them regularly.","They are covered in tiny hooks.","Water forms droplets that roll off and carry dirt away."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"تأثير اللوتس."},{"n":8,"s":"In paragraph 5, the word 'that' in 'tiny tooth-like scales that reduce drag' refers to","o":["sharks","bacteria","scales","ships"],"k":2,"ty":"reference","e":"The scales reduce drag.","h":"تعود على القشور."},{"n":9,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a difficulty of biomimicry?","o":["Natural structures often work at small scales.","Natural materials are difficult to reproduce.","Nature-inspired products are not always environmentally friendly.","Nature's designs are protected by law."],"k":3,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Legal protection is not mentioned.","h":"الحماية القانونية غير مذكورة."},{"n":10,"s":"What is the writer's overall attitude towards biomimicry?","o":["strongly critical","completely uninterested","uncertain whether it has any value","positive, while recognizing its limits"],"k":3,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"Successes are presented with limitations.","h":"إيجابي مع إدراك الحدود."}]},{"t":"Microloans in developing economies","p":["For millions of people in poor countries, the main obstacle to improving their lives is not a lack of ideas or effort but a lack of money. A woman who sells vegetables in a village market might double her income if she could buy a cart, or a tailor might expand his business with a second sewing machine. Yet traditional banks have often refused to lend to such people. Their loans are too small to be profitable, they rarely have property to offer as security, and many live far from any bank branch.","Microfinance was developed to address this problem. Its best-known pioneer is Muhammad Yunus, a Bangladeshi economics professor. In 1976, after lending small sums from his own pocket to a group of women in a village near his university, he discovered that almost all of them repaid the money on time. This experience led him to establish the Grameen Bank, which provides small loans, known as microloans, to poor borrowers, most of them women. In 2006, Yunus and the Grameen Bank were jointly awarded the Nobel Peace Prize.","A key feature of the Grameen model is group lending. Borrowers form small groups, and although each person receives an individual loan, the group meets regularly and members encourage one another to repay. If one member fails to repay, the others may lose access to future loans. This social pressure partly replaces the property that banks traditionally require as security, and it has helped many microfinance institutions to achieve very high repayment rates. Loans are usually repaid in small weekly instalments, which borrowers find easier to manage than a single large payment at the end.","During the 1990s and early 2000s, microfinance spread rapidly across Asia, Africa and Latin America, and it was widely praised as a powerful tool for reducing poverty. Supporters pointed to individual success stories and argued that microloans gave women, in particular, greater independence and influence within their families and communities.","However, more careful research produced a more mixed picture. Between 2009 and 2015, several large studies compared communities that received access to microloans with similar communities that did not. They found that microloans helped some existing businesses to grow and gave households more flexibility in managing their money, but on average, they did not dramatically increase incomes or lift large numbers of people out of poverty. In some places, problems also emerged. When commercial lenders entered the market seeking high profits, some charged very high interest rates, and borrowers took out several loans at once, leading to serious debt.","Today, most experts view microfinance as useful but limited. Many organizations now offer a wider range of financial services, including savings accounts, insurance and mobile payments, which may be just as valuable as loans for poor families. In some countries, Islamic microfinance has also developed, using profit-sharing and cost-plus arrangements instead of interest. The lesson of the past few decades is that access to finance can help people to improve their lives, but it is not, on its own, a solution to poverty."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which of the following is the best title for the passage?","o":["How to Open a Bank Account","The Life of a Village Tailor","Small Loans, Mixed Results","Why Banks Make Large Profits"],"k":2,"ty":"best title","e":"The passage describes microloans and their mixed impact.","h":"قروض صغيرة ونتائج متفاوتة."},{"n":12,"s":"Why have traditional banks often refused to lend to poor people?","o":["Their loans are too small to be profitable and they lack security.","Poor people never repay loans.","Governments do not allow it.","Poor people do not want loans."],"k":0,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 1 gives the reasons.","h":"قروض غير مربحة وبلا ضمان."},{"n":13,"s":"What did Yunus discover from his first small loans?","o":["Most borrowers could not repay.","Banks were willing to help him.","Almost all the women repaid on time.","Men were better borrowers than women."],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 states this.","h":"أعادت النساء القروض."},{"n":14,"s":"The word 'security' in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to","o":["protection from criminals","property promised in case a loan is not repaid","a safe place to live","a guarantee of employment"],"k":1,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Banks require collateral.","h":"ضمان."},{"n":15,"s":"How does group lending help to ensure repayment?","o":["Each group shares a single bank account.","The government repays any missing money.","Members must give the bank their property.","Group members encourage one another, and everyone risks losing future loans."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"الضغط الاجتماعي."},{"n":16,"s":"In paragraph 3, the word 'This' in 'This social pressure' refers to","o":["the property required by banks","the Nobel Peace Prize","the high interest rates","members encouraging one another and the risk of losing loans"],"k":3,"ty":"reference","e":"It refers to the group dynamics described before.","h":"الضغط داخل المجموعة."},{"n":17,"s":"What did the large studies between 2009 and 2015 find?","o":["Microloans helped some businesses but did not greatly increase average incomes.","Microloans ended poverty in most communities.","Microloans had no effect at all.","Microloans were used mainly by men."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 5 summarizes the results.","h":"أثر محدود."},{"n":18,"s":"What can be inferred about commercial lenders in some places?","o":["They charged no interest at all.","They were more interested in profit than in helping borrowers.","They refused to lend to women.","They were controlled by Yunus."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"They charged very high interest rates.","h":"اهتمام بالربح."},{"n":19,"s":"Why does the writer mention Islamic microfinance?","o":["to show that microfinance has failed","as an example of an alternative approach to lending without interest","to criticize the Grameen Bank","to describe insurance services"],"k":1,"ty":"purpose","e":"It uses profit-sharing instead of interest.","h":"بديل بلا فوائد."},{"n":20,"s":"What is the writer's main conclusion?","o":["Microfinance should be banned.","Microfinance is the best way to end poverty.","Microfinance can help but is not a complete solution to poverty.","Only women should receive loans."],"k":2,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"The final sentence states this.","h":"مفيد لكنه غير كافٍ."}]},{"t":"The collapse of the Classic Maya","p":["Between about 250 and 900 CE, the Maya of Central America built one of the most sophisticated civilizations of the ancient world. In the tropical lowlands of what is now Guatemala, Mexico and Belize, they constructed great cities, such as Tikal and Calakmul, with tall stone pyramids, palaces and plazas. They developed a complex writing system, an accurate calendar and advanced mathematics that included the concept of zero. At its height, the region may have supported several million people. Maya farmers grew maize, beans and squash, and in some areas they built raised fields and terraces to increase production. The cities were connected by raised stone roads and by trade networks that carried goods such as jade, salt, cotton and cacao across great distances.","Yet in the ninth century, something dramatic happened. Across the southern lowlands, one city after another stopped building monuments, and inscriptions recording the deeds of kings came to an end. Populations declined sharply, and within about a century, many great cities had been largely abandoned to the forest. This event, known as the Classic Maya collapse, has puzzled scholars for generations. Over the years, they have proposed many explanations, including disease, invasion, earthquakes and even a peasant revolt against the ruling classes, although most of these ideas are now considered unlikely to explain such a widespread decline.","Researchers now believe that there was no single cause. One important factor appears to have been climate. By studying layers of sediment from lake beds and mineral deposits in caves, scientists have found evidence of a series of severe droughts between about 800 and 1000 CE. Because the Maya depended heavily on rainfall for their crops and stored water in reservoirs, repeated droughts would have caused serious food shortages.","Environmental damage may have made matters worse. To clear land for farming and to produce lime plaster for buildings, which required burning large quantities of wood, the Maya cut down extensive areas of forest. Deforestation can reduce local rainfall and cause soil erosion, so the Maya may unintentionally have increased their vulnerability to drought.","Political and social factors were also important. The Maya world was divided into many competing city-states, and inscriptions show that warfare became increasingly frequent in the late Classic period. Kings claimed authority partly on the basis of their special relationship with the gods, and when droughts and famines continued despite ceremonies and sacrifices, people may have lost faith in their rulers. Trade routes may also have shifted, reducing the wealth of some cities.","It is important to note that the Maya people themselves did not disappear. In the northern Yucatán, cities such as Chichén Itzá continued to flourish for several more centuries, and millions of Maya people live in the region today and still speak Mayan languages. What collapsed was a particular political system based on powerful kings and large cities. For modern societies, the Maya case offers a sobering lesson about how climate change, environmental damage and political conflict can combine to produce a crisis that no single factor could have caused alone."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the Maya calendar and mathematics","how pyramids were built","modern life in Guatemala","the possible causes of the decline of Classic Maya cities"],"k":3,"ty":"main idea","e":"Most of the passage discusses the collapse.","h":"أسباب الانهيار."},{"n":22,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a Maya achievement?","o":["a complex writing system","an accurate calendar","the invention of iron tools","the concept of zero"],"k":2,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Iron tools are not mentioned.","h":"الأدوات الحديدية غير مذكورة."},{"n":23,"s":"What happened to many Maya cities in the ninth century?","o":["They were destroyed by an earthquake.","They were conquered by Europeans.","They became much larger.","They stopped building monuments and were largely abandoned."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 describes this.","h":"هُجرت المدن."},{"n":24,"s":"How have scientists found evidence of ancient droughts?","o":["by studying lake sediments and cave deposits","by reading Maya weather records","by measuring the size of pyramids","by interviewing local people"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"رواسب البحيرات والكهوف."},{"n":25,"s":"Why would droughts have been especially serious for the Maya?","o":["They lived beside large rivers.","They did not grow any crops.","They depended heavily on rainfall for crops and water.","They imported most of their food."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"اعتمادهم على الأمطار."},{"n":26,"s":"The word 'vulnerability' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["strength","wealth","the state of being easily harmed","knowledge"],"k":2,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"They became more easily harmed by drought.","h":"قابلية التضرر."},{"n":27,"s":"Why might people have lost faith in their rulers?","o":["Kings refused to perform ceremonies.","Ceremonies failed to end droughts and famines.","The rulers moved to the north.","Inscriptions criticized the kings."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"فشل الطقوس."},{"n":28,"s":"What does the writer emphasize in the final paragraph?","o":["The Maya people survived even though a political system collapsed.","All Maya people disappeared.","Chichén Itzá was abandoned first.","Mayan languages are no longer spoken."],"k":0,"ty":"insertion / paragraph function","e":"'the Maya people themselves did not disappear'.","h":"بقاء شعب المايا."},{"n":29,"s":"Why does the writer describe the Maya case as a 'sobering lesson'?","o":["It proves that cities are always dangerous.","It shows that writing systems are useless.","It suggests that climate never changes.","It shows how several problems together can cause a crisis."],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"Multiple factors combined.","h":"تجمّع العوامل."},{"n":30,"s":"How is the passage mainly organized?","o":["It compares two modern countries.","It describes an event and then examines several possible causes.","It tells the life story of one king.","It lists Maya cities in alphabetical order."],"k":1,"ty":"organization","e":"Paragraphs 3–5 each present a cause.","h":"حدث ثم أسباب."}]},{"t":"Spider silk research","p":["Spider silk is one of the most remarkable materials in nature. Weight for weight, some types of spider silk are stronger than steel, and they can stretch considerably before breaking. They are also lightweight, flexible and biodegradable. For decades, scientists and engineers have dreamed of producing spider silk on a large scale, imagining uses ranging from surgical stitches and artificial ligaments to lightweight protective clothing and fishing lines.","A single spider can produce up to seven different types of silk, each made in a separate gland and suited to a different purpose. The strongest, known as dragline silk, forms the frame of the web and the safety line on which the spider hangs. Another type, used for the spiral of the web, is extremely elastic and sticky, allowing it to absorb the energy of flying insects without breaking. The secret of silk's properties lies in its structure: long chains of proteins combine regions that are tightly organized, which provide strength, with looser regions that provide elasticity.","If spider silk is so useful, why don't we simply farm spiders, as we farm silkworms? The answer is that spiders are difficult to keep in large numbers. Silkworms have been domesticated for thousands of years; they live peacefully together and each one produces a long thread when it spins its cocoon. Spiders are very different. Most species are territorial, and if they are placed together, they tend to fight and even eat one another. Moreover, each spider produces only small amounts of silk. One famous exception proved the difficulty: in 2009, a golden cloth made from the silk of more than a million wild spiders in Madagascar was displayed in a London museum, but it took several years and dozens of workers to produce.","Scientists have therefore turned to genetic engineering. By inserting the genes responsible for silk proteins into other organisms, they have persuaded bacteria, yeast, plants and even silkworms to produce spider silk proteins. Silkworms modified in this way can spin fibres that combine the properties of both types of silk. Several companies have begun to produce artificial spider silk for products such as clothing and cosmetics.","However, significant challenges remain. Producing the proteins is only the first step; turning them into fibres as strong as natural silk is much harder. Spiders spin silk through a narrow duct in which the chemical environment and the pulling force are carefully controlled, causing the proteins to line up in exactly the right way. Reproducing this process in a factory has proved difficult, and many artificial fibres are still weaker than the real thing. Costs are also high compared with traditional synthetic materials such as nylon.","Nevertheless, researchers remain optimistic. Medical applications are particularly promising, because spider silk causes little reaction from the human immune system and is gradually broken down by the body. Experiments have tested it for repairing damaged nerves and as a material for wound dressings. If production problems can be solved, a material that spiders have used for hundreds of millions of years may become part of everyday life."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the properties of spider silk and efforts to produce it","how spiders catch insects","the history of silk clothing","dangerous spiders around the world"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage covers both topics.","h":"خصائص حرير العنكبوت وإنتاجه."},{"n":32,"s":"According to the passage, which type of silk forms the frame of the web?","o":["spiral silk","dragline silk","silkworm silk","sticky silk"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 states this.","h":"حرير الإطار."},{"n":33,"s":"What gives spider silk both strength and elasticity?","o":["a coating of sticky liquid","the large size of the spider","a mixture of tightly organized and looser protein regions","the temperature of the gland"],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"تركيب البروتين."},{"n":34,"s":"Why is it difficult to farm spiders?","o":["They are protected by law.","They tend to fight and produce little silk.","They need very cold conditions.","They only live in Madagascar."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"عدوانية وإنتاج قليل."},{"n":35,"s":"Why does the writer mention the golden cloth from Madagascar?","o":["to show how difficult it is to collect natural spider silk","to describe a famous fashion designer","to prove that spider farming is easy","to compare silk with steel"],"k":0,"ty":"purpose","e":"It needed a million spiders and years of work.","h":"إظهار الصعوبة."},{"n":36,"s":"The word 'persuaded' in paragraph 4 is used to suggest that scientists","o":["asked spiders politely","paid companies to make silk","argued with other researchers","caused organisms to do something they would not normally do"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"The organisms do not naturally make spider silk.","h":"جعلوها تنتج ما لا تنتجه طبيعيًا."},{"n":37,"s":"In paragraph 5, the word 'which' in 'a narrow duct in which the chemical environment' refers to","o":["the factory","the duct","the proteins","the fibre"],"k":1,"ty":"reference","e":"Conditions are controlled in the duct.","h":"تعود على القناة."},{"n":38,"s":"What can be inferred about artificial spider silk today?","o":["It does not yet fully match the quality of natural silk.","It is cheaper than nylon.","It is stronger than natural silk.","It has replaced all synthetic materials."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"Many fibres are still weaker.","h":"أضعف من الطبيعي."},{"n":39,"s":"Why are medical uses considered promising?","o":["Spider silk causes little immune reaction and breaks down gradually.","It is very cheap to produce.","It can cure infections.","It never breaks down in the body."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 6 explains this.","h":"متوافق حيويًا."},{"n":40,"s":"Which statement best summarizes the passage?","o":["Spider silk is too weak to be useful.","Spiders are easy to farm in large numbers.","Genetic engineering has solved every problem.","Spider silk has great potential, but producing it on a large scale remains challenging."],"k":3,"ty":"summary / conclusion","e":"It summarizes the main points.","h":"إمكانات كبيرة وتحديات."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"Seldom ______ a student work as hard as Nawaf did this year.","o":["we have seen","we saw","have we seen","saw we"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"have we seen — Seldom at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Seldom."},{"n":42,"s":"If I were you, I ______ the job offer before reading the contract carefully.","o":["won't accept","didn't accept","hadn't accepted","wouldn't accept"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"wouldn't accept — Second conditional advice.","h":"الشرط الثاني."},{"n":43,"s":"The keys must ______ while we were moving the furniture.","o":["have been lost","be lost","have lost","had been lost"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"have been lost — Past deduction, passive.","h":"استنتاج في الماضي."},{"n":44,"s":"______ the meeting ends early, we can visit the exhibition.","o":["Unless","Even though","Provided that","Despite"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Provided that — Provided that = only if.","h":"بشرط أن."},{"n":45,"s":"The report, ______ last month, contained several errors.","o":["publishing","was published","to publish","published"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"published — Reduced passive relative clause.","h":"اختصار جملة الصلة."},{"n":46,"s":"It was not until midnight ______ the rescue team reached the village.","o":["when","that","which","then"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"that — It was not until … that.","h":"It was not until … that."},{"n":47,"s":"She would have caught the train ______ she left home five minutes earlier.","o":["if","unless","so that","although"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"if — Third conditional (if-clause second).","h":"الشرط الثالث."},{"n":48,"s":"Little ______ that his decision would change his life forever.","o":["he knew","knew he","he did know","did he know"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"did he know — Little at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Little."},{"n":49,"s":"By next June, Huda ______ at the hospital for exactly ten years.","o":["will work","will have been working","is working","has been working"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"will have been working — Future perfect continuous.","h":"المستقبل التام المستمر."},{"n":50,"s":"There's no point ______ about the past; we need to plan for the future.","o":["to worry","worrying","worry","worried"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"worrying — There's no point + -ing.","h":"no point + -ing."},{"n":51,"s":"The town ______ we stayed during the holiday was famous for its gardens.","o":["which","whom","whose","in which"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"in which — Preposition + which for place.","h":"in which."},{"n":52,"s":"______ the rain, the farmers would have lost the whole harvest.","o":["But for","Except","Apart","Without of"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"But for — But for = if it hadn't been for.","h":"But for."},{"n":53,"s":"Hardly anyone came to the lecture, ______?","o":["didn't they","did he","did they","didn't he"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"did they — Negative meaning (hardly) → positive tag.","h":"سؤال ذيلي."},{"n":54,"s":"The new law requires that all drivers ______ a seat belt at all times.","o":["wears","wear","wearing","will wearing"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"wear — Subjunctive after require that.","h":"صيغة الطلب."},{"n":55,"s":"______ to the conference, she would have presented her findings.","o":["Had she been invited","If she invited","She had been invited","Were she invite"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Had she been invited — Inverted third conditional.","h":"Had + فاعل."},{"n":56,"s":"I'd prefer you ______ smoke in the house, please.","o":["don't","didn't","won't","not"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"didn't — would prefer + subject + past.","h":"would prefer."},{"n":57,"s":"Of the two proposals, the second one is clearly ______.","o":["the best","better than","the better","the more good"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"the better — Comparing two: the better.","h":"المقارنة بين اثنين."},{"n":58,"s":"The patient's condition has improved, so the operation ______ be necessary after all.","o":["may not","mustn't","can't to","needn't to"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"may not — may not = possibly not.","h":"may not."},{"n":59,"s":"Not a single mistake ______ in her final essay.","o":["the teacher found","did the teacher find","found the teacher","the teacher did find"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"did the teacher find — Negative phrase at the start: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد النفي."},{"n":60,"s":"We were made ______ the entire report again.","o":["rewrite","rewriting","rewritten","to rewrite"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to rewrite — Passive make + to-infinitive.","h":"be made to."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"The (A) majority of the (B) research (C) were (D) funded by the university.","o":["majority","research","were","funded"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"were — research is uncountable: was.","h":"research غير معدود."},{"n":62,"s":"(A) Although (B) his knowledge of history, he (C) failed the (D) exam.","o":["Although","his","failed","exam"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Although — Although needs a clause: Despite.","h":"Despite مع الاسم."},{"n":63,"s":"She (A) has (B) lived in Jeddah (C) since (D) ten years.","o":["has","lived","since","ten years"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"ten years — for ten years.","h":"for مع المدة."},{"n":64,"s":"If it (A) will rain tomorrow, (B) the trip (C) will be (D) cancelled.","o":["will rain","the","will be","cancelled"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"will rain — First conditional: If it rains.","h":"المضارع بعد If."},{"n":65,"s":"The man (A) who's car (B) was stolen (C) reported it (D) to the police.","o":["who's","was","reported","to"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"who's — Possession: whose.","h":"whose للملكية."},{"n":66,"s":"(A) Hardly had I (B) opened the door (C) when the phone (D) ring.","o":["Hardly","opened","when","ring"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"ring — Past simple: rang.","h":"الماضي rang."},{"n":67,"s":"Each (A) of the (B) candidates (C) have to give (D) a short speech.","o":["of","candidates","have","a"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"have — Each of + singular verb: has.","h":"Each of مفرد."},{"n":68,"s":"She (A) is (B) getting used to (C) live in a (D) big city.","o":["is","getting","live","big"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"live — get used to + -ing: living.","h":"get used to + -ing."},{"n":69,"s":"The (A) more you read, (B) the (C) better your vocabulary (D) becoming.","o":["more","the","better","becoming"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"becoming — becomes.","h":"becomes."},{"n":70,"s":"He (A) asked me where (B) did I (C) buy my (D) laptop.","o":["asked","did I","buy","laptop"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"did I — Reported question: where I bought.","h":"السؤال المنقول بلا قلب."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M23-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"What does the second man really think?","o":["The weather is too bad for a picnic.","The weather is perfect for a picnic.","The park is too crowded.","December is too cold for picnics."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"He is being sarcastic.","h":"سخرية: الطقس سيئ."}],"t":"A sarcastic comment about the weather","sc":["Situation: Two friends meet outside a building. (51 words)","Man: Beautiful day for a picnic, isn't it?","Man: Oh, absolutely. Forty-eight degrees and a sandstorm coming. Perfect conditions for sitting in the park.","Man: So I take it you'd rather go somewhere indoors?","Man: Let's just say I'd prefer the air-conditioned café to being buried in sand. We can have the picnic in December."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M23-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"What is the man suggesting about the soup?","o":["Something is missing from it.","It is too thick.","It is too spicy.","It is better than Grandma's."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"He hints that it lacks flavour.","h":"ينقصها شيء."}],"t":"A hint about a missing ingredient","sc":["Situation: A man is tasting soup that his sister has made. (59 words)","Woman: Well? How's the lentil soup? It's my first time making Grandma's recipe.","Man: It's very good. The texture is perfect. It's just that Grandma's always had a bit more … flavour, somehow.","Woman: More flavour? I followed the recipe exactly. Oh, wait. I forgot the cumin!","Man: That might be it. Add a teaspoon now and let it simmer for a few minutes."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M23-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"What is the professor's main concern about the essay?","o":["It contains too few sources.","Its ideas are not original.","Its main argument is not clear until the end.","It is much too short."],"k":2,"ty":"opinion","e":"'the main point only appears at the very end'.","h":"الفكرة الرئيسة متأخرة."}],"t":"A professor suggests rewriting an essay","sc":["Situation: A student speaks to her professor. (76 words)","Woman: Dr. Ahmed, did you have a chance to look at my essay draft?","Man: I did. You've clearly done a lot of reading, and some of your ideas are very original. However, the argument is hard to follow, because the main point only appears at the very end.","Woman: Should I move it to the introduction?","Man: I'd suggest restructuring the whole essay around it. State your thesis clearly at the start and make sure each paragraph supports it."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M23-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"What does the woman ask the man to do instead?","o":["check the figures after she finishes","prepare the figures himself","explain the format to her","finish her other work"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"'Perhaps you could check them once I've finished'.","h":"مراجعة الأرقام لاحقًا."}],"t":"An offer of help that is declined","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are talking in an office. (70 words)","Man: You look busy. Would you like me to take care of the monthly sales figures for you?","Woman: That's very kind, but I think I'd better do them myself. I've already started, and the format is a bit complicated to explain.","Man: Are you sure? I've finished my own work for today.","Woman: I appreciate it. Perhaps you could check them once I've finished, though. A second pair of eyes is always useful."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M23-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"What is the second man implying?","o":["The report is too long.","The director will not read it.","The design should be changed.","The report contains a calculation error."],"k":3,"ty":"inference","e":"He questions the total on page twelve.","h":"خطأ في الحساب."}],"t":"A colleague's hint about a report error","sc":["Situation: Two colleagues are discussing a report. (58 words)","Man: I've sent the annual report to the director. I'm quite proud of it.","Man: It looks very professional. By the way, the total on page twelve seemed a little high. Did you include the cancelled orders?","Man: The cancelled orders? Oh no. I think I did.","Man: It might be worth sending a corrected version before the director reads it closely."]},{"rec":6,"a":"M23-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"What do the women decide?","o":["to go into the café now","never to go to the café","to complain to the manager","to try the café in a month's time"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"'Let's give it a try next month'.","h":"التجربة الشهر القادم."}],"t":"Doubts about a new café","sc":["Situation: Two friends are walking past a new café. (57 words)","Woman: Look, that new café has opened. Shall we try it?","Woman: Hmm. I've heard mixed reports. Apparently, the coffee's fine, but it's extremely expensive and the service is slow.","Woman: It's always like that in the first few weeks, though. They're still training their staff.","Woman: That's true. Let's give it a try next month, once things have settled down."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M23-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"What will the second man do?","o":["ask the landlord for a specific time","repair the leak himself","miss his lectures","move to a new flat"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"'I'll reply and ask him to give us a time window'.","h":"طلب وقت محدد."}],"t":"An unclear message from a landlord","sc":["Situation: Two flatmates are reading a message. (64 words)","Man: The landlord says someone will come 'to look at the water' on Tuesday. What does that mean?","Man: Probably the leak under the kitchen sink we reported last week.","Man: But he didn't give a time. One of us will need to be here.","Man: I'll reply and ask him to give us a time window. I have lectures all morning, but I'm free after one o'clock."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M23-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"What does the receptionist offer?","o":["an immediate room change","a full refund","a room in another hotel","a room change tomorrow and a free dinner tonight Longer conversations"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"He offers both.","h":"تغيير الغرفة غدًا وعشاء مجاني."}],"t":"A disappointing hotel view","sc":["Situation: A guest speaks to a hotel receptionist. (63 words)","Woman: Excuse me, I booked a room with a sea view, but my window looks directly onto the car park.","Man: I'm very sorry. Let me check. You're right, there's been a mistake with the booking.","Woman: Is it possible to change rooms?","Man: All our sea-view rooms are full tonight, but I can move you tomorrow morning. And tonight's dinner will be complimentary, as an apology."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M23-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"Why do they want to create a simple website first?","o":["because apps are not allowed at the university","because tutors prefer websites","to test the idea cheaply before building the app","to sell advertising"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"A minimum viable product tests the idea cheaply.","h":"اختبار الفكرة بتكلفة قليلة."},{"n":80,"s":"How do they plan to make money?","o":["by charging students a membership fee","by selling the app to the university","by taking a small percentage of each session","by paying tutors less"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'take a small percentage from each session'.","h":"نسبة من كل جلسة."}],"t":"Planning a student start-up","sc":["Situation: Two students are planning a business. (180 words)","Man: So, we agree that the idea is an app that connects university students with tutors from higher years?","Man: Yes. Students often struggle to find reliable help, and senior students would welcome the extra income.","Man: The first thing we need is evidence that people actually want it. Before we spend money on developing the app, let's survey students in our faculty.","Man: Good idea. We could also create a simple version first, perhaps just a website with a booking form, and see how many people use it.","Man: That's what business experts call a minimum viable product. It lets you test the idea cheaply.","Man: How would we make money? Charging students might put them off.","Man: We could take a small percentage from each session, maybe ten per cent. The tutors would set their own prices.","Man: And the university's innovation centre offers free advice and small grants to student start-ups. We should apply.","Man: Definitely. The deadline is at the end of the month. I'll prepare the survey, and you can look at the grant application.","Man: Deal. Let's meet again on Thursday to review everything."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M23-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"What does the supervisor say Sara should improve?","o":["the quality of her research","speaking up in meetings","her relationships with colleagues","her punctuality"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"'Mainly confidence in meetings'.","h":"الثقة في الاجتماعات."},{"n":82,"s":"What can be inferred about the supervisor's view of Sara?","o":["She would consider employing Sara in the future.","She thinks Sara's research was weak.","She does not want to write a reference.","She thinks Sara talks too much."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"She mentions a possible opening next year.","h":"قد توظفها مستقبلًا."}],"t":"Discussing an internship evaluation","sc":["Situation: An intern meets her supervisor. (185 words)","Woman: Thank you for coming in, Sara. As your internship is ending, I'd like to go through your evaluation.","Woman: Of course. I'm a little nervous.","Woman: There's no need. Overall, it's very positive. Your research for the marketing team was thorough, and your colleagues found you reliable and easy to work with.","Woman: That's great to hear. Is there anything I should improve?","Woman: Mainly confidence in meetings. You often had good ideas, but you shared them with me privately afterwards instead of speaking up. Your ideas deserve to be heard by the whole team.","Woman: I know. I worry that my suggestions might sound silly.","Woman: That's very common. One technique is to prepare one or two points before each meeting, so you feel ready to contribute.","Woman: I'll try that. Would you be willing to write me a reference for graduate positions?","Woman: I'd be happy to. In fact, we may have an opening in the team next year, so keep in touch.","Woman: Thank you so much. I've learned a great deal here, especially about analysing customer data.","Woman: You're welcome. Remember to add that experience to your CV, with specific examples."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M23-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"Why is travel to Al-Ahsa less of a problem than expected?","o":["The university will pay for flights.","They can stay with a relative there.","Al-Ahsa is very close.","They will film online."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"'My uncle lives there'.","h":"الإقامة عند العم."},{"n":84,"s":"Why do they reject the documentary about delivery drivers?","o":["It would be too expensive.","Similar documentaries have already been made.","Drivers refused to take part.","It is too dangerous to film. Talks and lectures"],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"Originality matters for the mark.","h":"تكرار الفكرة."}],"t":"Choosing a topic for a documentary","sc":["Situation: Two media students are discussing a project. (183 words)","Man: We need to choose a topic for our ten-minute documentary. Any ideas?","Man: I was thinking about the traditional crafts in Al-Ahsa, like palm weaving and pottery. Many of the craftspeople are elderly, and the skills might disappear.","Man: That's a strong subject, and it would look beautiful on film. But Al-Ahsa is a long way from here. Could we afford the travel?","Man: My uncle lives there, so we could stay with him. We'd only need fuel and food.","Man: That helps. My idea was a documentary about food delivery drivers in our city: their working conditions and why they do the job.","Man: That's interesting too, and much easier to film. But a lot of students have already made similar documentaries.","Man: Fair point. Originality counts for twenty per cent of the mark.","Man: So shall we go with the crafts?","Man: Yes, but let's contact the craftspeople first and make sure they're willing to be filmed. Without their agreement, there's no documentary.","Man: I'll ask my uncle to introduce us this week. He knows several of the potters personally.","Man: Excellent. Then we can plan the filming schedule next week."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M23-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"Why was wild wheat difficult for humans to harvest?","o":["It grew only in water.","Its grains were poisonous.","Its seeds fell to the ground when ripe.","It ripened only once every ten years."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"The head of the plant shatters.","h":"تتساقط البذور."},{"n":86,"s":"How do archaeologists distinguish domesticated grains from wild ones?","o":["by the colour of the grain","by the rough scar on the base of the grain","by the size of the field","by the age of the sickles"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Domesticated grains show a rough scar.","h":"الندبة الخشنة."},{"n":87,"s":"What does the lecturer say about the speed of domestication?","o":["It happened within a single year.","It took about a hundred years.","It probably took two or three thousand years.","It has never been studied."],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"'perhaps two or three thousand years'.","h":"استغرق آلاف السنين."}],"t":"Extended lecture: the domestication of wheat","sc":["Situation: An archaeology lecturer speaks to students. (298 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Today, we'll look at one of the most important developments in human history: the domestication of wheat. For most of our existence, humans lived as hunter-gatherers, moving from place to place to find wild plants and animals. Around eleven thousand years ago, in a region of the Middle East known as the Fertile Crescent, which stretches from the Levant through southern Turkey to Iraq, people began to cultivate plants deliberately. Wheat was among the first. Wild wheat has a feature that makes it difficult to harvest. When the grains are ripe, the head of the plant shatters, scattering the seeds on the ground. This is useful for the plant, because it spreads its seeds, but inconvenient for humans. However, a few plants carried a genetic mutation that prevented shattering, so their grains stayed on the stalk. When people harvested wheat with sickles, these non-shattering plants were more likely to be collected, and when some of the collected grain was planted the following year, the mutation became more common. Over many generations, without necessarily intending to, people created wheat that depended on humans to spread its seeds. Archaeologists can trace this process by examining ancient grains found at sites such as Abu Hureyra in Syria. The broken base of a wild grain looks smooth, while a domesticated grain shows a rough scar where it was separated by threshing. Interestingly, the process seems to have taken a long time, perhaps two or three thousand years, rather than happening suddenly. The consequences were enormous. Farming allowed people to produce surplus food and to settle permanently, which led to larger communities, the growth of towns and, eventually, the first cities and states. Wheat remains one of the world's most important crops, providing about a fifth of the calories humans consume."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M23-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"Why is the moment magnitude scale now preferred to the Richter scale?","o":["It is easier for the public to understand.","It measures damage to buildings.","It was developed earlier.","It works better for large and distant earthquakes."],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"The Richter scale had limitations.","h":"أدق للزلازل الكبيرة والبعيدة."},{"n":89,"s":"How much more energy does a magnitude seven earthquake release than a magnitude five?","o":["about twice as much","about ten times more","about a hundred times less","about a thousand times more"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"نحو ألف ضعف."},{"n":90,"s":"What is the difference between magnitude and intensity?","o":["Intensity measures the total energy released.","Intensity describes the effects of shaking at a particular place.","Magnitude changes from place to place.","They are two names for the same measurement."],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"One magnitude, many intensities.","h":"الشدة تصف الأثر المحلي."}],"t":"Lecture: how earthquakes are measured","sc":["Situation: A geology lecturer speaks to students. (299 words)","Lecturer: Good afternoon. When an earthquake occurs, news reports usually give a number describing its size. Today, I'd like to explain what these numbers mean and how scientists measure earthquakes. Earthquakes happen when stress builds up along faults, which are fractures in the Earth's crust, and the rocks suddenly slip. This releases energy in the form of seismic waves, which travel through the Earth and are recorded by instruments called seismometers. For many years, the most famous measure was the Richter scale, developed by Charles Richter in 1935. It was based on the size of the waves recorded on a particular type of seismometer. However, the Richter scale had limitations: it did not work well for very large earthquakes or for those far from the instrument. Today, seismologists generally use the moment magnitude scale, which is based on the total energy released, calculated from the area of the fault that slipped and the distance it moved. One important point is that both scales are logarithmic. This means that each whole number represents about ten times more ground movement and roughly thirty-two times more energy than the number below it. So a magnitude seven earthquake releases about a thousand times more energy than a magnitude five. Magnitude, however, does not tell us everything. Scientists also measure intensity, which describes the effects of shaking at a particular place, such as damage to buildings and how strongly people felt it. A single earthquake has one magnitude but many intensities, because shaking is usually stronger near the source and in areas with soft soil. In Saudi Arabia, the national seismic network monitors activity, particularly along the Red Sea, where the African and Arabian plates are slowly moving apart. Accurate measurement helps engineers design safer buildings and helps authorities respond quickly when an earthquake strikes."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Electric cars offer several important advantages.","Petrol prices change every month.","Many cities have heavy traffic.","Some electric cars are made in China."],"x":["______ First, they reduce air pollution because they produce no exhaust. Second, they are cheaper to run than petrol cars. Finally, they are quieter, which reduces noise in cities."],"k":0,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details list advantages.","h":"مزايا السيارات الكهربائية."},{"n":92,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Some people sleep with the lights on.","Exams are usually held in the morning.","During sleep, the brain organizes and stores the information we have learned during the day.","Coffee contains caffeine."],"x":["Sleep is essential for learning. ______ For this reason, students who stay up all night before an exam often perform worse than those who sleep well."],"k":2,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"It explains why sleep matters before 'For this reason'.","h":"تفسير قبل For this reason."},{"n":93,"s":"The (A) children (B) was (C) delighted with their (D) presents.","o":["children","was","delighted","presents"],"k":1,"ty":"error","e":"children is plural: were.","h":"children جمع."},{"n":94,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["The southern region of Jazan and Asir offers a very different experience from the rest of Saudi Arabia.","Saudi Arabia is a large country.","Honey is good for health.","Many people travel in summer."],"x":["______ Hikers can explore green mountains and terraced farms, while visitors in summer enjoy temperatures far cooler than in the rest of the country. The region is also famous for its coffee and honey."],"k":0,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details describe a distinctive region.","h":"تجربة مختلفة في الجنوب."},{"n":95,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Some businesses sell clothes online.","However, they often lack experience in managing money and planning for difficulties.","Universities offer many courses.","Business meetings can be long."],"x":["Many young people want to start their own businesses. ______ As a result, a large number of new businesses close within their first few years."],"k":1,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"A problem is needed before 'As a result'.","h":"مشكلة قبل As a result."},{"n":96,"s":"The scientist (A) was (B) awarded the prize for (C) discover a new (D) species.","o":["was","awarded","discover","species"],"k":2,"ty":"error","e":"for + -ing: discovering.","h":"for + -ing."},{"n":97,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"x":["(1) Recycling helps to protect the environment. (2) It reduces the amount of waste sent to landfill. (3) It saves energy and raw materials. (4) Plastic was invented in the nineteenth century. (5) It can also create jobs in the recycling industry."],"k":2,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"The history of plastic does not develop the point about recycling.","h":"تاريخ البلاستيك خارج الفكرة."},{"n":98,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 1 – 3 – 4","3 – 2 – 1 – 4","2 – 3 – 4 – 1","2 – 3 – 1 – 4"],"x":["(1) Then, they began to keep animals such as sheep and goats.","(2) At first, people lived by hunting and gathering wild plants.","(3) Later, they learned to plant crops near rivers.","(4) Eventually, permanent villages grew into the first towns."],"k":3,"ty":"best order","e":"At first → Later → Then → Eventually.","h":"التسلسل الزمني."},{"n":99,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["English is spoken in many countries.","Clearly, the effort of learning a language brings rewards that last a lifetime.","Some languages have difficult grammar.","Many people travel abroad."],"x":["Learning a second language has many advantages. It improves job opportunities, allows people to communicate with others from different cultures and may even keep the brain healthy in old age. ______"],"k":1,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"It sums up the advantages.","h":"خلاصة الفوائد."},{"n":100,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["However","Otherwise","Instead","Furthermore Teacher Section — Mock 23"],"x":["The museum's new exhibition attracted thousands of visitors. ______, it received excellent reviews in national newspapers."],"k":3,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"It adds another positive point.","h":"Furthermore للإضافة."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
